First off you may not have FTP'ed it with the correct transfer mode. 
To download it from "ISIB" you must use the 'tenex' transfer mode. 
See the sample transfer log below:

*tenex
<Type L bytesize 32 ok.
*get HACK351.ARC.1 hack.tnx
<Port 20.87 at host 26.6.0.87 accepted.
<Retrieve of <INFO-IBMPC>HACK351.ARC.1 started.
257432 bytes in 339 seconds--6072 bps
<Transfer completed. 64358 (32) bytes transferred.
*

Note that HACK351.ARC.1 was transfered using logical bytesize of 32,
not just the binary mode. So you can try this, and see if you have
better results.

Secondly, I read that the Unix version of ARC was chock full of bugs.
The author of Unix ARC (actually the one who attempted to port it to
Unix) posted an article to some newsgroup saying not to use it at all,
and that work on a good port was progressing (I think). So you should
only use ARC under MS-DOS.

Thirdly, you need to transfer HACK351.ARC down to your micro correctly.
You must use some form of binary transfer (ie: kermit, xmodem, etc).
I prefer to use xmodem. There was a pair of xmodem programs for Unix
on net.sources some time ago. They were called "sb" and "rb", presumably
for send/recieve binary. The mod.sources archives may have them.

